    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of different starting lineups possible, we start by noting that there are 12 players on the team and two of them, Ace and Zeppo, are already decided to be in the starting lineup. This leaves us with 10 players who can be selected from the remaining 10 players.\n\nWe need to choose 3 more players from these 10 remaining players. The number of ways to choose 3 players from 10 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\nHere, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\), so we have:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3!7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{3! \\times 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{720}{6} = 120\n\\]\n\nSo, the number of different starting lineups possible is \\( \\boxed{120} \\).",
